	        	<div id="350841733219030189-blog" class="blog-body" style="float: left;"> 
		<div id="wsite-content">	<div id="blog-post-267998584324054261" class="blog-post">
	
	
		<div class="blog-header">
			<h2 class="blog-title">
					<a class="blog-title-link blog-link" href="//www.alancavagnaro.com/blog/your-towns-future-is-being-decided-without-you-i-know-because-im-in-the-room">Your Town’s Future Is Being Decided Without You. I Know, Because I'm in the Room.</a>

			</h2>
			<p class="blog-date">
					<span class="date-text">
		7/21/2025
	</span>

			</p>
			<p class="blog-comments">
					<a href="//www.alancavagnaro.com/blog/your-towns-future-is-being-decided-without-you-i-know-because-im-in-the-room#comments" class="blog-link">
		0 Comments
	</a>

			</p>
		</div>
	
		<div class="blog-separator">&nbsp;</div>
	
		<div class="blog-content">
				<div class="paragraph">&#8203;From outsider to elected official, I&rsquo;ve seen how power works, and who gets excluded.</div>  <div><div class="wsite-image wsite-image-border-none " style="padding-top:10px;padding-bottom:10px;margin-left:0px;margin-right:0px;text-align:center"> <a> <img src="/uploads/2/3/5/3/23532730/old-lyme-zoning-4-9-25_orig.jpg" alt="Picture" style="width:auto;max-width:100%" /> </a> <div style="display:block;font-size:90%">Old Lyme residents showed up in force at the April 9th, 2025, zoning meeting to oppose new homes.</div> </div></div>  <div class="paragraph"><br /><span style="color:rgb(42, 42, 42)">A new report from last week confirms what many of us already suspected: land-use boards across Connecticut are wildly unrepresentative of the communities they serve. And that has real consequences on our statewide housing stock.</span><br /><br /><font color="#2a2a2a"><span>According to&nbsp;</span><a href="https://cho.thehousingcollective.org/impact/participation-local-control-land-use-connecticut">new research from the Housing Collective</a><span>, a non-profit group based in Connecticut, over 90% of land-use board members in Fairfield and New London Counties are white. The majority are men. The median age is 60 to 65. Most are homeowners. Meanwhile, renters, younger people, and families of color, those most impacted by Connecticut&rsquo;s housing shortage, are essentially removed from the room where zoning decisions are made. They do not have a seat at the table, and that is a problem.</span><br /><br /><span>This is not just a numbers problem. It&rsquo;s a power problem. When land-use boards are dominated by people with similar backgrounds and life experiences, their decisions are shaped by a narrow set of interests, often to preserve the norm, block new housing, and keep neighborhoods the way they have always been. Exclusive, with no door open. How is the generation of tomorrow supposed to find a home when today&rsquo;s leaders control what is and is not available?</span></font><br /><br /><font color="#2a2a2a">Let&rsquo;s face it. There are structural reasons why wealthy, white, male individuals are often overrepresented on local zoning boards. These positions tend to attract those with the time, resources, and incentives to preserve the status quo, rather than opening the door.</font><br /><br /><font color="#2a2a2a">In contrast, working parents, like a single mother juggling multiple jobs, or a full-time college student, may not have the flexibility to attend lengthy meetings or run for local office. In communities where zoning board seats require election, the time and effort needed to campaign can further discourage participation. Why is it important for working class people to be involved in the discussion, too, you must ask?</font><br /><br /><font color="#2a2a2a">When policies are shared for the few, they do not reflect the needs of the many. In my hometown, I&rsquo;ve heard more than one person claim that &ldquo;only homeowners should serve on zoning.&rdquo; But here&rsquo;s the truth: renters live in our communities, too. So do students. So do seniors on fixed incomes, and families just trying to get by. Everyone who calls a place home deserves a voice in shaping it.</font><br /><br /><font color="#2a2a2a"><span>We need more people at the table who understand what it&rsquo;s like to struggle to find a home, not just those focused on protecting what they already have.</span><br /><br /><span>As an elected land use commissioner from South Windsor, CT, it is imperative that the people who serve on these local boards represent the community as a whole as they shape the outcomes for entire communities. Broadening participation ensures that local decisions reflect the full range of community needs, notably when those decisions influence where people can live, raise a family, or settle down.</span></font><br /><br /><font color="#2a2a2a"><a href="https://projects.iq.harvard.edu/sites/projects.iq.harvard.edu/files/pegroup/files/foster_warren_nimby_problem.pdf">Local zoning boards have historically played a key role in blocking new development</a><span>, especially multi-family housing, a practice often referred to as &ldquo;Not In My Backyard,&rdquo; or NIMBYism. Even former&nbsp;</span><a href="https://www.cnn.com/2025/07/14/politics/obama-democrats-message">President Barack Obama has spoken out against this mindset</a><span>, calling attention to its shortcomings and the need for more inclusive, abundant housing policies.</span></font><br /><br /><font color="#2a2a2a"><span>NIMBYs obstruct the political process and act in bad faith.&nbsp;</span><a href="https://www.cato.org/commentary/never-underestimate-nimbyisms-impact-economic-growth">Our local economies hurt when new development is halted arbitrarily</a><span>. At that point, the local tax base thins, and we have one group to thank for that.</span></font><br /><br /><font color="#2a2a2a">Zoning boards with elected members, perhaps counterintuitively, are often more dominated by individuals primarily focused on protecting their own property values and neighborhoods. Appointed commissioners, however, are selected by town councils or political parties, usually bringing a broader perspective shaped by policy priorities or community-wide goals. It is up to you to break that mold and change the status quo.</font><br /><br /><font color="#2a2a2a">The good news? There&rsquo;s a real opportunity to act. Across states, such as Connecticut, countless amounts of land-use board seats are either vacant or expired, many quietly falling through the cracks in our communities. By actively recruiting from the full diversity of our communities, we help ensure these boards better reflect the people they serve.<br />&#8203;</font><br /><font color="#2a2a2a"><span>You can also do what I did, and challenge the political establishment and run for your local land use board, if your community allows it.</span><br /><br /><span>So here&rsquo;s your homework: If we want a community that is reflective of us all, it starts with who is serving at the table. Consider getting involved with your local land-use board, local town committee, or encouraging someone you trust to do so, because if you&rsquo;re not at the table, someone else will be speaking on your behalf.</span></font><br /><br /><em><font color="#2a2a2a">The official and updated article can be found on Next City by clicking <a href="https://nextcity.org/urbanist-news/your-towns-future-is-being-decided-without-you.-i-know-because-im-in-the-ro" target="_blank">here.</a></font></em></div>

